WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:05.440
Episode 2 of "Loki" left us with a pretty
big cliffhanger - and after Episode 3, we

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.279
have even more questions.

00:00:07.279 --> 00:00:11.330
The latest episode definitely gave MCU fans
plenty to unpack.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:15.770
Here's everything about the plot and ending
of "Loki" Episode 3, explained.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:20.610
Surprisingly, the episode doesn't kick things
off right from the moment Loki and the Variant

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:26.109
step through the Time Door in RoxxCart - instead,
we get the Variant and Hunter C-20, out for

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:27.109
drinks.

00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:31.570
The two are drinking margaritas in a supposedly
scummy waterfront restaurant, which is all

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:36.480
an elaborate illusion so the Variant can extract
information about the TVA from C-20.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.590
As we learn towards the episode's end, this
"Inception"-style technique is one of the

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:46.640
Variant's impressive skills that Loki doesn't
seem to have - different Loki, different powers.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:50.940
With this enchanting skill, it makes the Variant
a pretty formidable opponent, because how

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.621
would anyone know what's real if she can make
them believe they're somewhere else just to

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:56.720
get secrets and information out of them?

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:01.630
Anyway, thanks to a sleepy confession from
Hunter C-20, the Variant knows that the elevators

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:05.339
that go to the Time Keepers' chamber are behind
a set of gold doors.

00:01:05.339 --> 00:01:09.509
It's still not clear what she'd do if she
came face-to-face with the Space Lizards - the

00:01:09.509 --> 00:01:14.330
insinuation is that she'd kill them, but it
seems as if her plans go a little deeper than

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:15.330
that.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.530
Before anything about the Variant's plan can
be revealed, Loki pulls the villain through

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:22.549
another Time Door before Judge Renslayer can
execute them both.

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:26.960
Loki may have saved their lives - but he also
stranded the duo in yet another apocalypse.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.070
Because Loki instinctively took the duo to
a new place in time, he didn't carefully choose

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:37.609
their destination, hurling them onto the doomed
moon of Lamentis-1 in 2077 and draining the

00:01:37.609 --> 00:01:41.249
power out of the time-travelling TemPad device
in the process.

00:01:41.249 --> 00:01:46.130
While this location isn't a well-known cosmic
setting in the comics or the MCU like Xandar

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:51.969
or Sakaar, the planet Lamentis is featured
in the 2007 "Annihilation Conquest: Prologue"

00:01:51.969 --> 00:01:52.969
comics.

00:01:52.969 --> 00:01:56.969
Is setting part of "Loki" on Lamentis' moon
a small hint that alien baddie Annihilus is

00:01:56.969 --> 00:01:57.969
on the way?

00:01:57.969 --> 00:02:01.819
Maybe, but at this point it feels more like
an Easter Egg than a genuine tease for this

00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:03.930
longtime Marvel Comics villain.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:08.039
As Loki and the Variant arrive, the mining
colony and everyone living on the moon is

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:12.849
about to be wiped out by the approaching planet
above it, unless they can escape on a nearby

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:13.849
ship.

00:02:13.849 --> 00:02:18.000
After some illusion-trickery, the mischievous
duo sneak their way onto a train heading for

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.290
the evacuation craft, the Ark.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:24.340
With some time to kill, the terrible twosome
chat about their lives and their respective

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:25.340
abilities.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:29.920
It's arguably the best scene in the episode,
because, like Loki's time-therapy conversations

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.470
in the first two episodes, it forces the trickster
to look at his own life from a different perspective.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:40.120
Meanwhile, audiences probably didn't expect
to hear what the God of Mischief thinks about

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:41.120
love.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.500
We've never seen him in a romantic relationship
in the MCU, but it's clear he's got some very

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:50.810
complicated feelings about love - comparing
it to a dagger, and noting that it can disappear

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:52.110
in an instant.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:57.230
Loki also reveals that he's bisexual in this
quiet, emotional scene, something later confirmed

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:59.219
by director Kate Herron on Twitter.

00:02:59.219 --> 00:03:03.650
Of course, he's already a queer character
within Norse mythology and is gender fluid

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:07.430
in the comics - but it's great that the MCU
has finally acknowledged this.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:12.599
Though many assumed that the blonde trickster
in "Loki" was Lady Loki, Episode 3 seemed

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:15.340
to reveal that this may not actually be the
case at all.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:17.420
"Are you sure you're a Loki?"

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:18.430
"You're in my way."

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:19.760
"You are my way!"

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.709
She constantly berates our trickster for referring
to her as a Loki, eventually sniping that

00:03:24.709 --> 00:03:29.579
her new alias is Sylvie - a detail that was
spoiled in the international credits of Episode

00:03:29.579 --> 00:03:30.760
2 a week earlier.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:33.829
Right about here is where things get complicated.

00:03:33.829 --> 00:03:39.159
Sylvie says Loki is "not who [she] is anymore,"
so it seems like the MCU is making her part

00:03:39.159 --> 00:03:42.760
Loki, and part another Marvel character: Enchantress.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:47.360
In the comics, there's a version of Enchantress
amed Sylvie Lushton, who is given Asgardian

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.500
abilities by Loki when the Norse gods move
Asgard to Oklahoma.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:55.540
Marvel Studios has always taken the stories
and details from the comics and put its own

00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:01.090
spin on things for the MCU, so it's entirely
possible that Kevin Feige and creator Michael

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:06.760
Waldron have combined Enchantress and Lady
Loki into one variant that honors both characters.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:11.499
However, Sylvie also starts revealing certain
puzzle pieces about her life before the events

00:04:11.499 --> 00:04:16.060
of the series, explaining that she's been
on the run from the TVA for her whole life.

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:20.440
If that's truly the case, then that raises
all sorts of questions about where in Loki's

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:25.090
timeline she may have actually come from - if
she's truly a Loki variant at all.

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:26.330
But that's not all she revealed.

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.760
Perhaps the biggest revelation in the episode
comes after the pair of Lokis are thrown off

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:32.289
the train heading to the Ark.

00:04:32.289 --> 00:04:37.889
As they traipse across Lamentis, Sylvie explains
that the mind of Hunter C-20 was "messed up"

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:42.540
and "clouded," noting that C-20 had to trawl
through a memory from hundreds of years ago,

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.770
from before she joined the TVA.

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:49.040
So far, viewers have been led to believe that
when the Time Keepers created the TVA, they

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:54.190
also created all the agents like Mobius and
Hunter C-20 - but apparently that's not the

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:55.380
truth at all.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:05:00.509
Sylvie shockingly reveals that everyone in
the TVA is a variant, just like herself and

00:05:00.509 --> 00:05:01.509
Loki.

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:04.810
So does that mean Mobius is obsessed with
jet skis because in his normal timeline he

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:05.960
liked them too?

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:08.740
Which timelines were all the TVA agents from?

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:13.590
How did the Time Keepers assemble them after
the Multiverse War into this cosmic bureaucracy?

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:15.190
And just how does Sylvie know all of this?

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:20.440
In "Loki" Episode 2, the series established
that time-travelers typically don't have any

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:24.830
effect on an apocalypse event because they're
fixed points in time, which means nothing

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:26.449
about it can be changed.

00:05:26.449 --> 00:05:30.539
So it does seem a little pointless that Loki
and Sylvie attempt to get on the evacuation

00:05:30.539 --> 00:05:31.919
ship in the first place.

00:05:31.919 --> 00:05:36.009
Obviously they can't just sit on Lamentis
and wait to die, but it was only last week

00:05:36.009 --> 00:05:41.220
that we watched Loki destroy Mobius' salad
to explain just how apocalypse events work.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:45.560
As such, it was more than just a long shot
that Loki and Sylvie would manage to succeed

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:46.560
here.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.160
The Ark was always destined to blow up, so
it isn't surprising that the episode left

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.650
them facing annihilation in the neon-drenched
city.

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:57.420
Sylvie hammers that home when they first land
there, noting that of all the apocalyptic

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:00.280
events they could've gone to, "this is the
worst one."

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:02.639
So, how will Loki get out of this one?

00:06:02.639 --> 00:06:05.530
Well, here's a theory: Lamentis-1 might not
be real.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:10.139
Look at the focus given to Sylvie's enchanting
abilities throughout the episode - what if

00:06:10.139 --> 00:06:14.070
she's simply put Loki into an illusion in
an attempt to get more information out of

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:15.100
him about the TVA?

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:19.699
Sure, it would undercut the entire drama and
stakes of this episode, but it would definitely

00:06:19.699 --> 00:06:21.360
be an interesting twist.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:25.610
That said, Sylvie claims the fantasies have
to come from the memories of the person she's

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:26.610
enchanting.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:30.870
"In order to preserve the connection, I have
to create a fantasy from their memories."

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:36.050
So, by that logic, she couldn't trap Loki
somewhere he's never been, unless this explanation

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:37.080
is also a deception.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:39.490
At this point, who knows?

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:43.550
Fans will just have to wait to see what happens
to Sylvie and Loki next week - new episodes

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:48.039
of "Loki" drop every Wednesday at midnight,
Pacific Time, on Disney Plus.

